    "case_id": "498",
    "case_summary": "A 19-year-old woman presented to the emergency department with nausea, vomiting, prodromal nonspecific visual symptoms followed by transient loss of consciousness lasting a few minutes. Parents reported spontaneously resolving generalized tonic-clonic convulsions. No prior medical history, denied substance abuse. Patient reported ongoing metrorrhagia three weeks after regular menses and denied sexual activity.\n\n- Increased body habitus (BMI 32)- Temperature 38.0°C- Blood pressure 155/95 mm Hg- Heart rate 104 beats per minute- Bilateral leg edema - Globular abdomen without tenderness or masses on palpation- Normal neurological examination- Normal fundoscopy\n\nLaboratory Tests:- Blood tests largely unremarkable- β-human chorionic gonadotropin: 16,140 IU/L (normal <10 IU/L)- α-fetoprotein: 43.5 kU/L (normal <5 kU/L) - CA-125: 131 kU/L (normal <35 kU/L)- Repeat β-HCG 24 hours later: 2,407 IU/LImaging Studies:- Head CT: Cystic lesion in third ventricle with upstream ventriculomegaly- Brain MRI: Arachnoid cyst of third ventricle, hypothalamic hamartoma, cortical and subcortical T2-hyperintensities- Abdominal ultrasound: Discretely enlarged uterus, ovaries not visualized due to obesity- EEG: Normal postictal electroencephalography",
